akr 0.1
project templartorrent

record templartorrent.decision.fork-rbit-for-native-gate/1 : decision {
    title "Fork rbit 0.2.2 in-tree to drop reqwest/rustls/ring; announce over an application-owned HTTP transport"
    state proposed
    scope [ path "Cargo.toml", path "integration/rbit-adapter/**", path "vendor/rbit/**" ]
    topic rbit-fork
    decision """
        Vendor rbit 0.2.2 into the repository as a path dependency applied through [patch.crates-io], make its reqwest dependency optional behind a Cargo feature that TemplarTorrent does not enable, and replace rbit::HttpTracker in the adapter with an application-owned bounded plain-HTTP announce client that connects to the already-admitted tracker address. HTTPS trackers are refused with a visible reason until a pure-Rust TLS stack is chosen.
        """
    context """
        The strict native-source audit reported ring 0.17.14 through rbit -> reqwest (rustls-tls) -> rustls -> ring. rbit 0.2.2 as published offers no feature to disable reqwest, which it uses in tracker/http.rs, webseed.rs and upnp.rs. The owner chose the fork option on 2026-10-22 over keeping the gate open or accepting ring as an exception.
        """
    consequences """
        The adapter still depends on the rbit API surface (peer connection, framing, metainfo, bencode, piece hashes) but the version pin becomes a pinned in-tree fork rather than the crates.io artifact, and rbit upgrades require re-applying the feature gate. HTTP tracker announces are now the application's responsibility, including bounded response reading and redirect refusal; webseed and UPnP from rbit are unavailable and were never used.
        """
    resolves [ @templartorrent.question.ring-release-gate ]
}
